<ArticleLayout
  title="How to stop saying “um” and “uh”"
  description="Filler words come from a timing gap between thought and speech. Learn four practical techniques to reduce them — and how to measure your progress objectively."
  path="/resources/reduce-filler-words"
  readingTime="5 min read"
>
  <p>
    "Um," "uh," "like," "you know," "basically" — filler words are the verbal tics that
    creep in when we speak under pressure. They're so automatic that most people have no
    idea how often they use them. The good news: fillers aren't a character flaw, and
    you don't need to be naturally eloquent to cut them. You just need to understand where
    they come from.
  </p>

  <h2>Why we use filler words</h2>
  <p>
    A filler is what comes out during the <strong>gap between finishing one thought and
    starting the next.</strong> Your brain hasn't queued the next phrase yet, but you feel
    pressure to keep making sound — so "um" fills the silence. Fillers are a
    <strong>timing problem</strong>, not a vocabulary problem. That's why the fix is about
    managing pauses, not learning fancier words.
  </p>

  <h2>Four techniques that actually work</h2>
  <h3>1. Embrace the silent pause</h3>
  <p>
    The single most effective fix is to <strong>replace the filler with silence.</strong>
    A one-second pause feels like an eternity to you but reads as confident and thoughtful
    to your listener. When you feel an "um" coming, close your mouth and let the silence
    sit. This one swap eliminates the majority of fillers.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Slow down on purpose</h3>
  <p>
    Most fillers appear when we speak faster than we can think. Drop your pace and the gaps
    your brain needs stop colliding with the pressure to make sound. Aim for a conversational
    <strong>110–160 words per minute</strong>.
  </p>
  <h3>3. Shorten your sentences</h3>
  <p>
    Long, winding sentences create more mid-thought gaps — and every gap is a filler
    opportunity. Make a point. Stop. Make the next point. Periods are your friend.
  </p>
  <h3>4. Hear yourself</h3>
  <p>
    You can't fix what you can't notice. The fastest way to reduce fillers is simply to
    become aware of them by <strong>recording yourself and counting</strong>. Awareness
    alone cuts usage dramatically, because your brain starts flagging the habit in real time.
  </p>

  <h2>Measure it, don't guess</h2>
  <p>
    Telling yourself "say fewer ums" doesn't work because you have no baseline. Instead,
    track a concrete number: your <strong>filler rate</strong> — fillers as a percentage of
    total words. Under 2% sounds polished; above 5% is noticeable to listeners. Watch that
    number trend down over a week and you have proof you're improving.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    What gets measured gets managed. A filler count you can see is worth more than a vague
    intention to "do better."
  </blockquote>
